{
  "requirement": "Each public SDK item has one defining module path. The SDK exposes no public\nre-exports. The chapter that introduces each supported type or free function owns its declaration.\nThe [public API inventory](sdk/public-api.md) links each item to that requirement,\nwhich describes its purpose\nand declares its public Rust interface, including fields, enum variants, trait\nmembers, and inherent methods. A change to that interface requires a change to\nthe owning requirement. Every supported struct with public fields is\n`#[non_exhaustive]`. Its constructor accepts the values needed to create it.\nAdditional fields have consuming builder methods that accept a concrete value.\nOptional fields start as `None`. Backend inference options start empty and can\nbe added one key at a time.\n\nThe SDK’s public modules follow the interfaces introduced in these chapters.\n\n- `domain`: application data and the `Domain`, `NodeContents`, and `SourceNodes` traits.\n- `graph`: criterion definitions, graph observations, and evidence; `graph::validator`\n  runs criteria.\n- `request`: `ValidationContext`, typed `Ask` builders, context values, and request phases.\n- `agent`: configuration for the supplied agents.\n- `sources`: project inspection, search queries, and their results.\n- `outline`: selectable source contents and language rules; `outline::languages`\n  supplies bundled languages, and `outline::selector` defines selector syntax.\n- `storage`: filesystem evidence storage.\n\nSource access, search engines, redaction, provider transports, agent-result paging,\nand conversation machinery are implementation details of those interfaces.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the reviewed tests adequately check `requirements.sdk.public_api`. I found no unacknowledged gap.\n\n**Obligations and the tests that cover them**\n\n1. **One defining module path and no public re-exports.**\n   - `tests/public_api.rs#fn[sdk_has_no_public_reexports]` parses every `.rs` file under `src` with tree-sitter. It fails on any `use_declaration` with a plain `pub` visibility modifier.\n   - `public_exports_match_the_appendix` also checks the rustdoc output. It walks rustdoc's published item tables and compares the `(path, kind)` pairs with the inventory in `md/requirements/sdk/public-api.md`. It asserts there are no missing or unexpected entries and no duplicates. An item reachable by a second module path would show up as unexpected.\n\n2. **The owning chapter declares each item, and the declared interface is the actual one.**\n   - `public_exports_match_the_appendix` calls `documented_exports`, which calls `check_declaration` for every non-module item.\n   - `specified_exports` checks that each declaration link points at a narrative chapter and that the requirement anchor matches that chapter's namespace.\n   - `check_declaration` reads the owning `req` block and requires a description and a Rust listing.\n   - It compares trait implementations, the indexed member names, and the normalized declaration plus inherent method signatures against rustdoc's output. That covers fields, variants, trait members and inherent methods, and it fails on any drift in either direction. This enforces \"a change requires a change to the owning requirement\".\n   - `member_order_is_free_but_field_and_method_types_are_committed` tests the comparison helper itself. Reordering methods is accepted. Changing a field type, a return type, or `&mut self` to `&self` is rejected.\n\n3. **Every struct with public fields is `#[non_exhaustive]`.** `check_declaration` asserts that the rustdoc declaration contains `#[non_exhaustive]` for any struct page that has public struct fields.\n\n4. **Constructor, consuming builders and concrete values.** The signature comparison pins the constructor parameters and the `self`-by-value builder signatures with concrete (non-`Option`) parameter types. Behavioural tests back this up:\n   - `agent/config.rs#mod[tests]/fn[backend_builders_set_only_the_requested_fields]` exercises every builder on `BedrockConfig`, `OpenAiConfig`, `OllamaConfig` and `BedrockCredentials`. It asserts exactly which fields are set after each chain.\n   - `source_bytes_error_kinds_and_language_names_round_trip` covers `SourceFile::new` and its `language` builder.\n   - `scoped_text_results_keep_line_numbers_context_and_completeness` covers the `TextQuery` builders `scope`, `context_lines` and `limit`.\n   - `built_in_languages_and_structural_search_use_the_project_scope` covers the `StructuralQuery` builders `scope` and `limit`.\n\n5. **Optional fields start as `None`.**\n   - The config test serializes freshly built configs. `BedrockConfig::new` gives `{\"model\":…}` and `OpenAiConfig::new` gives `{}`. Optional fields are skipped when `None`, so this shows they start unset.\n   - `SourceFile` asserts `language == None`. `TextQuery` and `StructuralQuery` assert `limit == None`.\n   - `Segment::ordinal` is asserted `None` in `outline/selector.rs#mod[tests]/fn[parses_named_anonymous_and_ordinal_segments]`. That test is marked for the Segment requirement rather than this one, which is the owning-chapter arrangement. I do not count it as a gap.\n\n6. **Backend inference options start empty and are added one key at a time.** The config test shows `options` is absent when empty. It then adds `a`, `b`, and `a` again, and asserts the result is `{\"a\":2,\"b\":true}`. The same single-key `option` call is checked for the OpenAI and Ollama configs.\n\n7. **Module list.** The module list in the requirement text is enforced through the inventory and rustdoc comparison. A missing or extra public module fails `public_exports_match_the_appendix`.\n\n**Not assessed here.** Each owning requirement's own tests cover behaviour beyond the shape of the interface. This requirement only asks for that shape.\n\n**Gaps.** None found. No `{fixme}` acknowledgments exist or are needed.",
  "references": "1f6a63bba43a7144abcfe7cd798bf312674830f0f1259db427607f5c6160a0cb",
